  "messages": [
    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Choose one next planning step using the local decision below. Return exactly one listed statement and stop. No other planning choices have been supplied.\n{\"context\": \"Reserve shelves hold accepted items that do not yet fit on display, and table volunteers can request replenishment without changing anyone’s existing claim.\", \"decision\": \"How should reserve stock reach the display tables?\", \"goal\": \"Help usable household goods reach interested neighbors while keeping contributions, claims, and final collection understandable.\", \"problem\": \"Neighbors are organizing a one-day fair for clean, usable household goods that people no longer need. Giving and taking are both optional, and no payment or matching contribution is required. Volunteers have intake space, category tables, reserve shelves, a holding area, carts, and simple item records. Contributors choose in advance whether unclaimed goods should be returned during the closing collection period or donated through an arranged pickup.\"}\nCANDIDATE STATEMENTS:\nReserve volunteers prepare small mixed replenishment batches, and table volunteers collect them when their displays have room. Sorting happens near the stored goods while the people managing each table decide when additional stock can fit.\nTable volunteers request reserve goods when their displays develop gaps, and a runner brings the matching available items. Replenishment follows visible need at each table rather than moving stock that would immediately crowd the display.\nA runner checks the display tables at regular intervals and brings reserve goods that fit the available spaces. One repeating circuit keeps stock moving without requiring table volunteers to leave their visitors or send individual requests."
    }
  ],
